nes. Instead Black filled in on the left side of the cut, and White's stones at E3 and E4 stayed connected."}, {"turn": 11, "text": "Black's F3 was the biggest loss of the game, 17.2 points, dropping the lead from -14.7 to -31.9. The engine preferred E2. F3 only pushed against White's E3 and E4 stones from the side."}, {"turn": 12, "text": "White's F5 lost 19.9 points, the worst move of the game, and the score went from -31.9 back to -12.0. The 14k had been given a huge lead and handed most of it back. Black had a real chance here, and it was never fully taken."}, {"turn": 22, "text": "White's C6 lost 7.8 points and moved the score from -12.9 to -5.2, the closest Black came to catching up. The game was still open, and the 14k's loose play had brought Black back within reach."}, {"turn": 33, "text": "Black's B9 lost 12.5 points, from -8.3 to -20.9. The board shows Black's edge group on the upper left squeezed against a White wall along row 5, with A5 and A6 already taken. The engine's choice was A9."}, {"turn": 35, "text": "Black's E9 lost 12.1 points, sinking the score from -20.1 to -32.3. After this the top-left group was in trouble: the final board shows White holding A9, C9, D9, E9 and F9 along the top, with nine Black stones captured."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberty count, count.py scores area with named dead stones removed, and the final command only appended game notes to CLAUDE.md. Each works on the current position or on records, and none ranks candidate moves or looks ahead.", "stop": false}}